WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.160
this is a this is a really
interesting session about how you know

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:08.040
what happens at a, at a larger enterprise
when, when skills really are distributed.

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:13.040
And there are many, many development teams
wanting to wanting to use skills.

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:17.280
It really poses kind of different
challenges at a larger organization.

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:22.040
So John, who's a principal engineer
at Cisco, will be will be presenting this.

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:22.800
I actually presented

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:26.640
with John a little while earlier at Cisco
Live down in Amsterdam.

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:28.800
So it gives me great pleasure to welcome
John on stage.

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:31.800
So please give it up for John.

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:41.840
All right.

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:43.680
So hopefully everyone can hear me
back there.

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:47.720
So again the title here is about skills
and putting them everywhere and really

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:49.120
putting pipelines around that.

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:52.680
So you don't have to maintain multiple
versions of context in various places.

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:53.520
Because again,

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:57.120
the end of the day, both humans and agents
really need to consume this context.

00:00:57.160 --> 00:00:58.720
So I'm going to kind of talk
about some things

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:02.240
we've been struggling with and some
patterns we've used internally at Cisco.

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:06.120
So this might be a little controversial
for some people here,

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:09.240
especially maybe if you work
for a frontier model and be earmuffs it

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:11.880
but you don't really need a smarter
model today.

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:15.800
The models are perfectly capable of doing
what you need for business value, right?

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:20.160
Not talking like PhD level intelligence
and beyond, but getting business value

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:22.160
out of the models. It's already there
today.

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:23.560
The problem is really that you need

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:26.560
smarter context and smarter
context engineering.

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:31.080
And so for me, the last couple of years
I've been jumping harness to harness.

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:34.640
I'm always chasing the latest, greatest
thing, constantly breaking my workflows.

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:36.400
I'm sure other people can relate to this.

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:40.440
And always there's so many variables
that anytime I switch to a lower model

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:43.360
and it breaks something, I'm immediately
like, well, that's the model's fault.

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:44.480
I'm going back to opus, right?

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:47.680
Because this thing that I don't
understand, it has to be the model, right?

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:50.800
But once skills came onto the scene,
it kind of changed my perspective

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:54.360
perspective on things,
especially recently with the new patterns

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:58.360
of a agentic fan out in the harness, right
where I have my model set to opus,

00:01:58.360 --> 00:02:02.600
and I ask it to do this massive prompt
and it spawns 15 sub in.

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:06.040
That cost is just really increasing,
and we're actually seeing that becoming

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:10.080
actually a concern where we had to start
limiting model use on our large enterprise

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:14.320
because of the cost of this stuff
and the fan out being a problem.

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:16.280
So I really wanted to figure out,

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:18.560
okay, why can't I just use sonnet
for everything?

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:20.200
And I found out I can.

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:23.440
And today for the last couple of months,
I largely used the medium tier model.

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:24.440
Doesn't really sonnet

00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:27.600
GPT medium reasoning
I don't really use those high end models.

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:29.520
It's very rare that I use those.

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:32.240
Maybe for some super complex planning.
Lots of contexts.

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:35.240
I still use them,
but I challenge my engineers to really try

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:38.840
to use that medium tier as their baseline
and only jump into the greater tier

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:39.600
when they need to.

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:43.840
And the real unlock for this was skills,
because all the harnesses,

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:46.600
all the models are training on skills,
they're honoring skills.

00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:48.440
And so if you can lean into that,

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:51.360
your context can actually transfer
between the harnesses.

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:53.200
And that was great for me because I can

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:57.320
I can try Claude Code, I can jump into dev
and I can jump into GitHub Copilot CLI.

00:02:57.320 --> 00:03:00.880
And if my skills work in all three of them
relatively, it works the same.

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:02.440
So it's great.

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:06.440
But what was what you need to understand
is how do you manage your context, right?

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:07.400
How do you share it?

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:08.240
Package it once

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:11.800
and then ship it to everyone,
but only have one source of management.

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:14.480
And the real unlock for that is evals.

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:17.120
Evals are extremely important,
and you need to make sure

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:19.280
that you're actually spending
engineering effort

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:21.600
learning how to build a valuations
for your context.

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:25.680
And what you'll find is if you do that
correctly, you can actually use

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:29.360
extremely low end models in your workflows
become much more deterministic,

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:32.960
especially for things that are repetitive
that you need to do all the time.

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:35.960
I don't think anyone in this room
if I want them and I said, hey, I can

00:03:35.960 --> 00:03:39.720
you can use opus and do this workflow,
or you can use haiku

00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:42.840
and one way faster and way cheaper,
but you get the exact same result.

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:45.000
I mean, which one are you going to choose?
It's a no brainer.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:45.720
No one's going to choose

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:49.080
the more expensive, slower model
just because if you actually figure out

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:52.560
how to do that optimization, you're going
to go with the cheaper, faster one.

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:54.440
So the stories
that I'm going to tell today

00:03:54.440 --> 00:03:58.240
stem from kind of that journey
of two completely different teams.

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:02.440
One is more of a traditional context
already exist that humans maintain.

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:04.000
How do we convert that into something

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:04.960
agents can maintain

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:09.000
without reinventing it, or having complete
drift in the two systems?

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:12.720
And then the second one is more developer
centric, where everything we do

00:04:12.760 --> 00:04:17.000
should be more skill based going forward
and kind of being the starting point.

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:20.720
So before we go too deep in
just a little background on myself,

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:23.840
My name is John Groetzinger,
and I've been in Cisco

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:25.320
for about 14 years now,

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:28.720
actually came in through an acquisition
of a company called Sourcefire.

00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:31.120
We wrote an intrusion prevention software.

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:35.000
It's still one of the core technologies
in Cisco's firewall today.

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:35.440
Right.

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:40.000
And so I spent about 12 years in the TAC,
which is our technical

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:43.560
assistance center, technical support
for enterprise customers.

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:46.840
I'd be dealing with very high pressure
situations financial

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:50.440
institutions, banks,
hospitals, critical infrastructure.

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:51.200
When that goes down,

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:54.480
I'd be the kind of person figuring out
how do we get them up quickly, you know,

00:04:54.520 --> 00:04:56.720
and I really want to automate that
because those are very

00:04:56.720 --> 00:04:58.280
annoying situations, right?

00:04:58.280 --> 00:05:02.760
And so I've been a full stack developer
for the last 12 to 14 years,

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:06.560
but the last 2 to 5 years,
there's a lot of edge cases that you can't

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:09.560
handle an automation right? Easily anyway.

00:05:09.560 --> 00:05:11.960
And AI kind of changed that a lot, right?

00:05:11.960 --> 00:05:13.360
I really got excited and I was like,

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:16.000
oh man, these 50 edge cases,
I don't have to hardcode.

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:18.280
If else is for
I can just throw it to an agent

00:05:18.280 --> 00:05:20.480
and with some context
and it actually handles that quite well.

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:25.800
And then for the last year or so, I've
been focused heavily on production system.

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:30.360
We just released a couple of weeks ago,
GA we have a fully AI native

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:34.480
platform built from the ground up to be
AI native, its customer external facing.

00:05:34.520 --> 00:05:38.960
We have over 1000 users on board in
just a few weeks, and it's been a that's

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:42.160
where these stories are actually coming
from its shipping production agents.

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:44.120
Okay, so

00:05:44.120 --> 00:05:47.480
let's take a step back and hopefully
everyone knows what a skill is.

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:50.560
If not, you probably haven't been paying
attention over the last couple of days.

00:05:50.600 --> 00:05:52.720
And so I really hope you have.

00:05:52.720 --> 00:05:57.480
But a core of it is it can be very simple
or it can be very complex, which is nice.

00:05:57.520 --> 00:06:00.600
It's flexible, but the bare minimum is
just you have that skill

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:04.120
that just is like your starting point
for your agent.

00:06:04.120 --> 00:06:07.080
And so one skill, for example,
that I've created for

00:06:07.080 --> 00:06:08.960
our team is a repository standard.

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:10.920
Because we have tons of technical debt.

00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:14.680
We have people creating new projects
all the time on using models

00:06:14.680 --> 00:06:17.040
that are trained on stuff
that's not quite up to date, right?

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:21.160
So if I say creating a new Python project,
it pumps out a requirements.txt

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:24.880
with a pip install and I go insane because
I only want people using UV, right?

00:06:24.920 --> 00:06:26.760
And so I put that into the skill

00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:30.520
so I don't have to have my engineers worry
about like setting up projects

00:06:30.520 --> 00:06:33.400
the wrong way. They just install the skill
and set up the project with the agent.

00:06:33.400 --> 00:06:35.120
So that's just one example.

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:36.480
That's an easy win, right?

00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:39.160
So you want to make your skill ship
with rules,

00:06:39.160 --> 00:06:41.480
which is essentially just more markdown
files.

00:06:41.480 --> 00:06:42.840
You can put scripts in there.

00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:46.040
Any scaffolding you want
can really be as good as you want,

00:06:46.040 --> 00:06:49.520
but what's key is that
you add a value for your skills, right?

00:06:49.600 --> 00:06:52.360
Define the bare minimum
that you expect this skill

00:06:52.360 --> 00:06:54.600
to always help with and always do it well.

00:06:54.600 --> 00:06:56.080
And so as you increase

00:06:56.080 --> 00:06:58.160
or improve the skill
or you add more to it,

00:06:58.160 --> 00:07:00.040
you don't want that old functionality
to break, right?

00:07:00.040 --> 00:07:03.600
So you need to focus on what is the core
stuff that should always do well.

00:07:03.640 --> 00:07:04.440
It's going to handle

00:07:04.440 --> 00:07:07.720
those other edge cases decently well,
and you can update the evals as those

00:07:07.720 --> 00:07:08.960
come out.

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:11.240
Now these are not really case studies.

00:07:11.240 --> 00:07:12.960
This is not like, hey,
you must go do this.

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:14.360
This is the way forward.

00:07:14.360 --> 00:07:15.240
These are really just

00:07:15.240 --> 00:07:19.480
some patterns we've tried because again,
one team is a development shipping team.

00:07:19.480 --> 00:07:22.760
We have extremely tight deadlines
because our executive leadership

00:07:22.760 --> 00:07:23.760
says you have AI now.

00:07:23.760 --> 00:07:26.440
You can deliver way more features
just as fast, right?

00:07:26.440 --> 00:07:27.640
That the world we live in.

00:07:27.640 --> 00:07:30.240
Not quite, but
that is the expectation that we live in.

00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:34.360
So we don't really have time to engineer
our workflows day to day, unfortunately.

00:07:34.360 --> 00:07:35.080
I wish we did.

00:07:35.080 --> 00:07:36.480
And then our support teams,

00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:40.160
same thing that under a lot of pressure
have high severity cases coming in.

00:07:40.200 --> 00:07:42.400
You know, I really have to figure out

00:07:42.400 --> 00:07:45.600
how to spend their time on
focusing on the customers and not really

00:07:45.640 --> 00:07:46.840
optimizing their day to day.

00:07:46.840 --> 00:07:49.720
So a lot of anecdotal stuff in here,
but these are just some patterns

00:07:49.720 --> 00:07:52.320
I wanted to share
that might be useful for you guys.

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:56.720
So story
number one comes from the support team.

00:07:56.760 --> 00:07:56.960
Right.

00:07:56.960 --> 00:08:00.720
And if you have an existing platform
of content for humans,

00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:03.360
how do you take that
and make it usable for agents?

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:05.840
It's kind of the story.

00:08:05.840 --> 00:08:09.440
So in our in Cisco TAC,
we have a very strong culture

00:08:09.440 --> 00:08:12.240
around our knowledge base,
something we instill in our culture

00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:14.680
because engineers
are constantly shifting out.

00:08:14.680 --> 00:08:16.960
The real value comes from that experience.

00:08:16.960 --> 00:08:19.520
I help this customer
with this really complicated problem.

00:08:19.520 --> 00:08:21.640
There's other ones that are
definitely going to have the same problem.

00:08:21.640 --> 00:08:22.520
Let me write that down

00:08:22.520 --> 00:08:25.000
so the next engineer doesn't
have to suffer like I did,

00:08:25.000 --> 00:08:27.320
and the customer doesn't
have to wait as long for a solution.

00:08:27.320 --> 00:08:28.960
It's just ingrained in our culture.

00:08:28.960 --> 00:08:32.400
So we really wanted to lean into
how do you leverage that which is highly

00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:35.960
curated, high quality content,
but feed it into an agent system?

00:08:36.200 --> 00:08:39.400
I think some of the first things we tried
were just given a tool

00:08:39.400 --> 00:08:42.960
to search the pull in the docs
and let the agent figure it out.

00:08:43.040 --> 00:08:44.440
Didn't really work so well.

00:08:44.440 --> 00:08:45.800
You know, there were wins here and there,

00:08:45.800 --> 00:08:48.480
but there were a lot of hallucinations
and stuff that just didn't make sense

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:52.240
because there is still stale
content in our knowledge based system.

00:08:52.280 --> 00:08:52.440
Right?

00:08:52.440 --> 00:08:53.880
Not every article is up to date,

00:08:53.880 --> 00:08:57.600
so you really need to pay attention
to which ones you let the agent consume.

00:08:57.680 --> 00:08:59.360
So two engineers that have been kind of

00:08:59.360 --> 00:09:01.600
working on this for two years,
a couple of months ago,

00:09:01.600 --> 00:09:04.600
created a platform
that converts our art articles

00:09:04.600 --> 00:09:07.280
which are kind of handpicked,
doesn't just blindly take every article

00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:10.560
and shove it into this agent system,
but high-quality articles

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:14.280
that people are actually actively
maintaining get converted into skills.

00:09:14.280 --> 00:09:16.880
And then we build evaluations
for that, right?

00:09:16.880 --> 00:09:19.600
So think customer opens a case.

00:09:19.600 --> 00:09:21.680
They have a problem description.

00:09:21.680 --> 00:09:23.480
We go through three weeks
of collecting logs

00:09:23.480 --> 00:09:24.840
and figuring out how to fix the problem.

00:09:24.840 --> 00:09:26.480
And then finally we fix it. Right.

00:09:26.480 --> 00:09:28.000
Why did it take three weeks?

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:31.720
How can we cut that time down into
just going from like description

00:09:31.720 --> 00:09:33.720
to solution on the first day? Right.

00:09:33.720 --> 00:09:35.720
And you'll write an article
to kind of help with that.

00:09:35.720 --> 00:09:39.000
And then we give it to our agent
so it can kind of automate that even more.

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:42.200
Now how many people here
have actually written the skill?

00:09:42.400 --> 00:09:44.400
Everyone. Most hands up. Okay.

00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:46.520
Now how many people have written
those skills?

00:09:46.520 --> 00:09:47.680
How many write them by hand?

00:09:47.680 --> 00:09:49.640
Do you write your skills out by hand?

00:09:49.640 --> 00:09:51.800
All right, a few people stop doing that.

00:09:51.800 --> 00:09:54.600
The skill is not for you.
The skills for your agent.

00:09:54.600 --> 00:09:56.480
So it's good to have human docs.

00:09:56.480 --> 00:09:59.120
But skill is for agents, right?

00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:00.000
It shouldn't.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:03.880
You should not really care what is inside
the skill, the context itself.

00:10:03.880 --> 00:10:06.160
What you should care about
is the outcome that comes out of

00:10:06.160 --> 00:10:08.000
using the skill with the agent.

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:11.840
So where your human spends the time,
that is your most precious resource

00:10:11.840 --> 00:10:15.640
as your humans, validating your taste,
the quality, what actually matters.

00:10:15.680 --> 00:10:16.600
That's the eval.

00:10:16.600 --> 00:10:19.120
And then the initial KB,
the stuff in the middle.

00:10:19.120 --> 00:10:20.400
Let the agent do it for you.

00:10:20.400 --> 00:10:22.120
Let LLMs do it for you.

00:10:22.120 --> 00:10:25.800
Even better, if you want a smaller model
to do this workflow, let the small model

00:10:25.840 --> 00:10:27.160
build the skill for itself.

00:10:27.160 --> 00:10:29.400
Found a lot of anecdotal success
in doing that.

00:10:29.400 --> 00:10:31.480
We're just kind of like, hey, well,
this is for you.

00:10:31.480 --> 00:10:33.560
How do you want to
organize this information

00:10:33.560 --> 00:10:35.120
so you can be more efficient with it?

00:10:35.120 --> 00:10:38.720
Just put that in a pipeline,
have the output quality as an eval,

00:10:38.720 --> 00:10:41.920
and you can kind of verify
if it's working or not based on that.

00:10:42.880 --> 00:10:46.000
So a lot of
people will say wikis over docs go to die.

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:48.440
And of course, largely
I do agree with that

00:10:48.440 --> 00:10:50.320
because people
just don't really maintain docs.

00:10:50.320 --> 00:10:54.200
But when you have a strong culture
like we do, our docs are not really dying

00:10:54.200 --> 00:10:55.680
or actually seeing the opposite.

00:10:55.680 --> 00:10:58.120
Our docs are becoming more important
to ground the agents,

00:10:58.120 --> 00:11:02.040
but what we're seeing is that a lot of
people are building a agentic systems

00:11:02.080 --> 00:11:06.120
that blindly try to use any content,
or they'll have agents that build

00:11:06.120 --> 00:11:09.360
their own memories on supporting stuff,
using that, and then the memories drift

00:11:09.360 --> 00:11:12.880
from the content, and then you end up
having some answer come out to a customer.

00:11:12.880 --> 00:11:16.560
And that's just completely unaligned
with the article that it says

00:11:16.560 --> 00:11:19.760
it uses a source, and then everyone
just loses faith in the entire system.

00:11:19.800 --> 00:11:20.000
Right?

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:21.560
And that's the problem
we've been struggling with

00:11:21.560 --> 00:11:22.760
for the last couple of years.

00:11:22.760 --> 00:11:23.560
So we're trying to figure out

00:11:23.560 --> 00:11:26.720
how do we keep these more in sync
in a way that we can actually trust it.

00:11:26.720 --> 00:11:28.120
And that's what the system

00:11:28.120 --> 00:11:31.120
that these engineers built
is that when the article changes,

00:11:31.160 --> 00:11:32.360
we kind of pipeline that, right.

00:11:32.360 --> 00:11:36.000
Think of like a GitHub action triggering
when we update a knowledge base article

00:11:36.040 --> 00:11:40.320
that goes into a pipeline with an LLM
that reviews the changes, determines

00:11:40.320 --> 00:11:44.080
if it's a minor change, moderate major,
and then makes the change for itself

00:11:44.080 --> 00:11:46.960
to update its skill.
And then it runs the evals.

00:11:46.960 --> 00:11:49.120
If the evals pass,
great, everything's good.

00:11:49.120 --> 00:11:51.640
If it's a minor change,
just changing a typo or wording

00:11:51.640 --> 00:11:54.720
and the evils pass,
I don't really need to have my human gate.

00:11:54.720 --> 00:11:57.520
That new version,
just let it automatically release.

00:11:57.520 --> 00:11:59.040
But if you're doing a more complex

00:11:59.040 --> 00:12:02.040
change, a major change,
maybe you're introducing a new topic.

00:12:02.080 --> 00:12:04.480
Maybe that's something that you need
some more quality control.

00:12:04.480 --> 00:12:06.320
And that's
where you insert your human right.

00:12:06.320 --> 00:12:09.840
Your humans either go far on the left
or far on the right, but in between

00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:13.640
you should not be wasting your time,
should not be caring about a text diff.

00:12:13.680 --> 00:12:15.040
If your engineers are reviewing

00:12:15.040 --> 00:12:17.640
text diffs on a skill,
you are wasting a lot of time.

00:12:17.640 --> 00:12:20.640
That is just unnecessary,
at least in my experience.

00:12:21.000 --> 00:12:23.680
So new topic means new eval, right?

00:12:23.680 --> 00:12:25.280
You need to build a new evaluation.

00:12:25.280 --> 00:12:28.320
Of course you can let the LM do that, but
you need to really tell it what success

00:12:28.320 --> 00:12:31.360
looks like in order for it
to build a good evaluation.

00:12:31.400 --> 00:12:32.160
Right?

00:12:33.880 --> 00:12:34.240
Okay.

00:12:34.240 --> 00:12:38.200
So that story number one, now story
number two is a little bit more complex.

00:12:38.200 --> 00:12:40.040
And it's more probably relatable

00:12:40.040 --> 00:12:42.440
to everyone in the room
because it's developer focused.

00:12:42.440 --> 00:12:46.920
And so this is a concept of just
how do you instill

00:12:46.920 --> 00:12:51.360
that culture of skill first context
engineering into your engineering teams.

00:12:51.360 --> 00:12:53.360
And that was something that we kind of

00:12:53.360 --> 00:12:55.920
did on our team
building this new agent platform.

00:12:55.920 --> 00:12:58.120
And so I'm going to kind of share
that story here.

00:12:58.120 --> 00:13:02.800
And so to talk a little bit about the
complexity and the environment working in,

00:13:02.880 --> 00:13:05.240
I want to tell you a little bit
about the system we built first.

00:13:05.240 --> 00:13:09.280
So the one that we released
a few weeks ago that I mentioned earlier,

00:13:09.360 --> 00:13:13.240
this AI native platform,
it's a multi-agent orchestration system.

00:13:13.280 --> 00:13:15.160
Of course, we have a central orchestrator.

00:13:15.160 --> 00:13:17.280
We don't call it an orchestrator
because we're Cisco.

00:13:17.280 --> 00:13:20.040
It's a router. It's a semantic router,
of course. Right.

00:13:20.040 --> 00:13:22.080
So we have our semantic router.

00:13:22.080 --> 00:13:23.800
It routes to multiple agents.

00:13:23.800 --> 00:13:26.360
There are on these eight specialist
agents.

00:13:26.360 --> 00:13:27.600
That's just what we have today.

00:13:27.600 --> 00:13:30.120
And we're already building
ten more of this quarter. Right.

00:13:30.120 --> 00:13:31.560
And that's constantly growing.

00:13:31.560 --> 00:13:34.000
So those are owned
by different development teams.

00:13:34.000 --> 00:13:35.840
Some of them are owned
by different organizations.

00:13:35.840 --> 00:13:38.360
But all of this goes into one
single platform.

00:13:38.360 --> 00:13:41.200
So the customer gets
one single unified interface.

00:13:41.200 --> 00:13:43.040
They have different apps
and different pages.

00:13:43.040 --> 00:13:44.400
They can navigate in there.

00:13:44.400 --> 00:13:47.920
There's whole pipelines
and databases behind each of those apps,

00:13:47.920 --> 00:13:49.920
and each of them
comes with their own agent.

00:13:49.920 --> 00:13:53.240
But we don't expose
that kind of complexity to the customer.

00:13:53.280 --> 00:13:54.200
That would be annoying.

00:13:54.200 --> 00:13:57.320
They have one AI assistant
that they can talk to everywhere.

00:13:57.520 --> 00:13:59.280
We have AI buttons embedded everywhere.

00:13:59.280 --> 00:14:02.600
Some are static buttons that are directly
routed to an agent to help them.

00:14:02.600 --> 00:14:04.480
But then we have free form chat as well.

00:14:04.480 --> 00:14:08.600
So if I'm sitting on the configuration
best practice app and I ask it

00:14:08.600 --> 00:14:12.000
what tack cases I have open that shouldn't
go to the configuration agent,

00:14:12.040 --> 00:14:15.080
it should go to the cases agent
and our system works well for that.

00:14:15.400 --> 00:14:17.560
The only way we were able to ship
that with confidence

00:14:17.560 --> 00:14:21.360
is because we did heavy evaluations,
and we have a strong engineering culture

00:14:21.360 --> 00:14:23.520
around that
now that we had to build. Right.

00:14:23.520 --> 00:14:26.600
And so that was something
that I was actually tasked

00:14:26.600 --> 00:14:29.240
with, was figuring out
how do we evaluate this stuff?

00:14:29.240 --> 00:14:32.680
I got the goal of saying, hey, you're
going to be the give the go or no, go on.

00:14:32.680 --> 00:14:34.760
If this is actually going
to be good enough to ship,

00:14:34.760 --> 00:14:37.280
because I think a lot of people
are stuck in the sea phase.

00:14:37.280 --> 00:14:38.600
So we need to be very confident

00:14:38.600 --> 00:14:41.080
that we're not going to release something
that's just terrible.

00:14:41.080 --> 00:14:43.600
We've seen all the disasters, people

00:14:43.600 --> 00:14:45.360
having agents
sell them a car for a dollar, right.

00:14:45.360 --> 00:14:49.040
We don't need that bad PR we really needed
to be confident about this.

00:14:49.240 --> 00:14:52.800
So I kind of came up with this framework
for testing, right?

00:14:52.880 --> 00:14:55.000
We used a lot of lang chain and lang
graph and Lang

00:14:55.000 --> 00:14:58.440
Smith for observing our agents,
which has a good eval culture.

00:14:58.440 --> 00:15:02.760
But the typical way that you create evals
and data sets today

00:15:02.800 --> 00:15:04.600
isn't really aligned with what I wanted,

00:15:04.600 --> 00:15:07.600
because I really wanted to be able
to compare all the environments.

00:15:07.640 --> 00:15:08.040
Right.

00:15:08.040 --> 00:15:12.520
So when you build a agentic platform,
your signals really come from your traces.

00:15:12.520 --> 00:15:15.200
You should ship early, monitor it,
and then adjust

00:15:15.200 --> 00:15:17.720
based on what you're observing,
how people interact with it.

00:15:17.720 --> 00:15:20.440
Because when you have a free form
chat, people can ask anything, right?

00:15:20.440 --> 00:15:22.240
So you can't really predict
what your people are going to.

00:15:22.240 --> 00:15:24.880
The people are going to ask,
kind of got to let them do it right.

00:15:24.880 --> 00:15:27.880
So you're going to be constantly
pulling evals from production data

00:15:27.880 --> 00:15:30.320
and then figuring out,
oh, that's a question they asked.

00:15:30.320 --> 00:15:33.600
Did not go well, one to the wrong agent,
even if it went to the right agent.

00:15:33.640 --> 00:15:35.000
Maybe that gave a bad answer.

00:15:35.000 --> 00:15:38.440
I want to build an evaluation for that
because it's something we want to support.

00:15:38.560 --> 00:15:42.120
Now, if I pull in this new eval
and I run it on my local laptop

00:15:42.120 --> 00:15:46.320
and I get it all working, okay, great,
that likely resulted in some

00:15:46.360 --> 00:15:50.240
sort of prompt change, some sort of tool
update, whatever it might be.

00:15:50.280 --> 00:15:52.880
What else did that break
to fix this one thing?

00:15:52.880 --> 00:15:54.480
That's the loop we constantly see.

00:15:54.480 --> 00:15:55.920
I'm chasing this current bug.

00:15:55.920 --> 00:15:58.240
I don't even know what that breaks
when I change the prompt.

00:15:58.240 --> 00:15:59.720
Without emails, you won't. Right?

00:15:59.720 --> 00:16:01.480
And so I had to figure out, okay,

00:16:01.480 --> 00:16:04.440
how do we actually do this
more unit test style, right?

00:16:04.440 --> 00:16:07.040
As an enterprise,
if you've ever scaled up software unit

00:16:07.040 --> 00:16:08.600
testing is absolutely essential.

00:16:08.600 --> 00:16:10.920
Evals
are the same in this world of the agentic.

00:16:10.920 --> 00:16:12.600
They are the unit tests for your agents.

00:16:12.600 --> 00:16:17.520
And so building this concept simple
things I wanted basic data set schemas.

00:16:17.520 --> 00:16:23.200
Everyone to use that same baseline schema
and and storing that with a repository.

00:16:23.440 --> 00:16:26.120
So the data set stored with the code.

00:16:26.120 --> 00:16:29.080
Now typically a data set is in JSON
format right.

00:16:29.080 --> 00:16:32.320
And usually a JSON
is just a single line file.

00:16:32.560 --> 00:16:35.440
So when an agent reads it,
if you have 1000 examples in there, it's

00:16:35.440 --> 00:16:38.040
just this massive context
that it explodes.

00:16:38.040 --> 00:16:40.280
So immediately I said, okay,
we need to switch.

00:16:40.280 --> 00:16:42.800
Everyone needs to be using JSON, L
instead of JSON.

00:16:42.800 --> 00:16:46.080
So then each example for each
eval is an individual line.

00:16:46.120 --> 00:16:49.680
Your coding agent can make more precise
edits on the data set.

00:16:49.760 --> 00:16:51.720
Because again, we're not hand curating
data sets.

00:16:51.720 --> 00:16:53.320
We absolutely don't have time for that.

00:16:53.320 --> 00:16:56.400
Everyone has a coding agent
and it's basically updating the data set.

00:16:56.440 --> 00:16:58.040
We need to make that more efficient.

00:16:58.040 --> 00:17:00.680
This is something I've learned
that I would instill into this skill.

00:17:00.680 --> 00:17:04.440
And I'm going to talk about
I wrote a script so this can work

00:17:04.440 --> 00:17:07.600
basically into your local laptop dev
staging the same exact

00:17:08.000 --> 00:17:11.560
same exact code works in any environment
because it's very simple.

00:17:11.600 --> 00:17:15.800
You know, traditionally when you have
a unit test, you import the function,

00:17:15.880 --> 00:17:19.040
you pass in whatever input you're testing,
and then you verify the output.

00:17:19.120 --> 00:17:19.760
Right.

00:17:19.760 --> 00:17:23.600
Agents are no different when you have
this kind of black box evaluation.

00:17:23.600 --> 00:17:25.160
You can do the exact same thing.

00:17:25.160 --> 00:17:28.720
However, what happened in the middle
you might actually care about as well.

00:17:28.760 --> 00:17:29.080
Right.

00:17:29.080 --> 00:17:31.640
And so you can actually observe that stuff
in a platform

00:17:31.640 --> 00:17:34.640
like Lang Smith or any system
observability system.

00:17:34.640 --> 00:17:36.800
That's
what our eval code actually runs against.

00:17:36.800 --> 00:17:38.200
So we don't import right.

00:17:38.200 --> 00:17:40.760
Like a Python import don't work like that.

00:17:40.760 --> 00:17:42.440
They get an endpoint. Right.

00:17:42.440 --> 00:17:45.960
And we point our evaluation code
at the endpoint to invoke the agent.

00:17:46.040 --> 00:17:47.200
Answer comes back out.

00:17:47.200 --> 00:17:49.040
We check the observability system.

00:17:49.040 --> 00:17:52.560
Did it call the tools we expected it
to call what the right parameters did it

00:17:52.800 --> 00:17:56.280
spend the roughly the amount of tokens
that we expected it to spend?

00:17:56.320 --> 00:17:58.880
What was the latency on this base
when we ran it last time? Right.

00:17:58.880 --> 00:18:01.920
You got to have a baseline
for all this stuff built into your evals.

00:18:02.000 --> 00:18:04.520
So all of this is what I'm trying
to basically teach

00:18:04.520 --> 00:18:09.000
all of my globally distributed team in
just basically have like a week or two

00:18:09.040 --> 00:18:12.000
to basically introduce
this concept to eight different teams

00:18:12.000 --> 00:18:13.760
distributed around the world.

00:18:13.760 --> 00:18:15.120
What options do I have?

00:18:15.120 --> 00:18:18.680
Get one meeting and get everyone on it
that they can just not pay attention to.

00:18:18.720 --> 00:18:21.040
I mean, let's be honest, what meeting

00:18:21.040 --> 00:18:24.720
is anyone going to pay attention to
for an hour of me at them about testing?

00:18:24.760 --> 00:18:26.400
No developer cares about this.

00:18:26.400 --> 00:18:28.360
It's another complex problem.

00:18:28.360 --> 00:18:30.080
Texting testing is not exciting.

00:18:30.080 --> 00:18:31.240
It's not exciting work.

00:18:31.240 --> 00:18:34.240
Engineers don't get excited about it,
so you got to do it for them

00:18:34.240 --> 00:18:35.320
as much as you can.

00:18:36.520 --> 00:18:38.000
How do you plug it into CI?

00:18:38.000 --> 00:18:40.600
How do you make it
environment aware right.

00:18:40.600 --> 00:18:42.320
How do we do the data set schema.

00:18:42.320 --> 00:18:44.800
All these things
we want to consistency around with?

00:18:44.800 --> 00:18:47.400
Again, hundreds of engineers
distributed around the world.

00:18:47.400 --> 00:18:49.760
So my thought was okay
meeting is not going to work.

00:18:49.760 --> 00:18:52.600
We have a little bit of time,
but everyone has a coding agent.

00:18:52.600 --> 00:18:56.320
And these development teams, the actual
developers, why don't we make a skill?

00:18:56.320 --> 00:18:58.520
And then I can just have everyone
install the skill

00:18:58.520 --> 00:19:00.360
and the agent
can build the evals for them.

00:19:00.360 --> 00:19:04.000
So that was basically the theory
and ended up working quite well.

00:19:04.000 --> 00:19:06.760
So the plugin that I built has our basic
skill.

00:19:06.760 --> 00:19:12.000
MD has a bunch of examples scripts,
example scripts, example evaluations.

00:19:12.040 --> 00:19:16.200
Basically, I spent a lot of time doing
this really well with one single agent

00:19:16.200 --> 00:19:18.240
to basically figure out
all these good patterns.

00:19:18.240 --> 00:19:21.240
And then I had my agent
to fill that down into a skill,

00:19:21.280 --> 00:19:24.520
basically explaining,
hey, everything that we just created here,

00:19:24.560 --> 00:19:26.480
can you make a skill for yourself,

00:19:26.480 --> 00:19:29.960
for other people with coding agents
to be able to do the exact same thing?

00:19:29.960 --> 00:19:31.600
And so that's what came out.

00:19:31.600 --> 00:19:33.520
The agent kind of built the skill for me.

00:19:33.520 --> 00:19:35.480
We iterated it with versions, right.

00:19:35.480 --> 00:19:37.320
And I shared it slowly with one team,

00:19:37.320 --> 00:19:41.000
got some feedback on day one, day
two and other team day three.

00:19:41.000 --> 00:19:42.000
Everybody. Right.

00:19:42.000 --> 00:19:44.120
And so teams just kind of installed it.

00:19:44.120 --> 00:19:45.080
They asked their coding agent,

00:19:45.080 --> 00:19:46.600
hey, do this thing
that I don't care about that

00:19:46.600 --> 00:19:48.800
John wants me to do,
and I have a job to do

00:19:48.800 --> 00:19:51.880
by the end of the week for right there
agent is kind of did it for them.

00:19:51.880 --> 00:19:54.080
And by the end of the week,
every agent team

00:19:54.080 --> 00:19:59.920
kind of had a data set done the same way,
similar metrics right as before this,

00:20:00.120 --> 00:20:03.440
if we just told eight teams
to go build evaluations for their agents,

00:20:03.520 --> 00:20:04.760
I mean,
what do you think would have happened?

00:20:04.760 --> 00:20:07.280
Right.
We would have had massive inconsistency.

00:20:07.280 --> 00:20:09.600
If I want to go help some other team,
I understand.

00:20:09.600 --> 00:20:12.320
Oh, their metric 0 to 10 and ten means
good.

00:20:12.320 --> 00:20:16.040
This team did 0 to 5 and five means bad,
and this team did 0 to 1.

00:20:16.080 --> 00:20:17.320
Like that is just something

00:20:17.320 --> 00:20:20.480
we have to speak the same language
and engineered towards the same goal.

00:20:20.480 --> 00:20:22.560
And a skill can really help
you kind of do that.

00:20:22.560 --> 00:20:26.080
So that's what I really wanted
everyone to contribute to this framework

00:20:26.080 --> 00:20:28.880
and we install it as a skill,
we version it and everyone

00:20:28.880 --> 00:20:30.720
kind of shipped it together.

00:20:30.720 --> 00:20:33.520
So that was essentially the theory
and what it worked.

00:20:33.520 --> 00:20:37.360
There was one problem that happened
is that the management

00:20:37.360 --> 00:20:40.640
would then come to me, my boss or whoever,
and they would say, hey,

00:20:40.680 --> 00:20:43.240
what's the update on that evaluation
framework?

00:20:43.240 --> 00:20:44.200
So-and-so said they liked it.

00:20:44.200 --> 00:20:45.920
Can you give me
can you show me what you did?

00:20:45.920 --> 00:20:47.800
And I was like,
oh yeah, you can just ask your coding.

00:20:47.800 --> 00:20:50.680
A you don't have one of those
because you're a manager.

00:20:50.680 --> 00:20:52.240
So that was a problem.

00:20:52.240 --> 00:20:54.320
Immediately I was like,
okay, there's still a gap here.

00:20:54.320 --> 00:20:56.840
But I wanted a very simple solution
for this because again,

00:20:56.840 --> 00:21:00.320
I don't my immediate reaction
or anyone's might be right.

00:21:00.440 --> 00:21:02.480
Please confluence wiki, whatever.

00:21:02.480 --> 00:21:05.480
I'll go put a document that explains
this eval framework that I built,

00:21:05.480 --> 00:21:07.520
and then I'll never update it
and it'll drift right.

00:21:07.520 --> 00:21:08.960
So I didn't want that.

00:21:08.960 --> 00:21:12.760
So the concept is really just
everything is in the repository.

00:21:12.800 --> 00:21:15.480
The skill is obviously backed
by gate. It's in a repo.

00:21:15.480 --> 00:21:17.040
Publish it to a registry like Tessl.

00:21:17.040 --> 00:21:20.720
But you're you maintain it in a git
repository that's version controlled.

00:21:20.720 --> 00:21:23.000
And you have a skill
that empty for an agent.

00:21:23.000 --> 00:21:26.200
And then you have a README.md
for a human right.

00:21:26.280 --> 00:21:27.120
Very simple.

00:21:27.120 --> 00:21:30.120
You can use an AI coding agent
to kind of update both, but you use

00:21:30.120 --> 00:21:33.160
the human kind of just validating
the readme and the skills for the agent.

00:21:33.160 --> 00:21:34.960
And you have evals
that make sure that works right.

00:21:34.960 --> 00:21:37.760
But not everyone gets on git either.

00:21:37.760 --> 00:21:39.360
A lot of our managers are not even in git.

00:21:39.360 --> 00:21:42.000
They operate solely
in like Jira and confluence.

00:21:42.000 --> 00:21:46.400
So I can't explain to 100 managers
go and go make an account on GitHub.

00:21:46.400 --> 00:21:48.920
And now you got to put this in confluence.

00:21:48.920 --> 00:21:51.240
So there's there's really cool
open source tools.

00:21:51.240 --> 00:21:54.560
You can build your own script
to basically convert that readme

00:21:54.560 --> 00:21:57.800
that's in the project directly
to just ship that to confluence.

00:21:57.840 --> 00:21:58.040
Right.

00:21:58.040 --> 00:22:01.280
You make a doc, think
hashtag one and markdown becomes H1.

00:22:01.520 --> 00:22:04.560
It's a very simple,
very deterministic conversion.

00:22:04.600 --> 00:22:06.400
Don't have an LLM, just update confluence

00:22:06.400 --> 00:22:08.840
I mean you can do that too,
but it's kind of token heavy

00:22:08.840 --> 00:22:11.200
just to have your agent write
a deterministic script to

00:22:11.200 --> 00:22:15.320
sync that markdown into a pretty HTML file
that everyone in that other system uses.

00:22:15.360 --> 00:22:16.960
And that was it. And that was all I did.

00:22:16.960 --> 00:22:19.000
So now when someone asked me for, hey,

00:22:19.000 --> 00:22:20.960
can you tell me about that agent framework
or I,

00:22:20.960 --> 00:22:24.880
they just ping me on WebEx and ask me,
where can I get information about this?

00:22:24.920 --> 00:22:28.720
I send them just a link
to that confluence doc, which is a direct

00:22:28.720 --> 00:22:30.760
1 to 1 map of the me from the repo.

00:22:30.760 --> 00:22:31.880
And so there's no drift.

00:22:31.880 --> 00:22:34.440
So what my agents are reading,
what their managers are reading

00:22:34.440 --> 00:22:37.120
is all the same content
and it's all maintained in one place.

00:22:37.120 --> 00:22:39.920
And that was really
just a very productive unlock.

00:22:39.920 --> 00:22:41.560
And it worked really well.

00:22:41.560 --> 00:22:44.320
And so the cultural shift
that I really have to

00:22:44.320 --> 00:22:47.120
instill in my team was asking first,
is this a skill?

00:22:47.120 --> 00:22:47.520
Right.

00:22:47.520 --> 00:22:51.080
So someone asks WebEx message like, hey,

00:22:51.240 --> 00:22:54.200
new engineers onboarding today,
where's the DevOps information?

00:22:54.200 --> 00:22:56.760
How do I get access to the Kubernetes
cluster? Right.

00:22:56.760 --> 00:22:59.000
It's not really
that's that is sometimes the question.

00:22:59.000 --> 00:23:00.680
And when I see that I say
no, no, no, no, no.

00:23:00.680 --> 00:23:03.000
Go ask
do we have a skill that explains this

00:23:03.000 --> 00:23:06.360
so I can install it in my agent
that needs to become the default reaction.

00:23:06.400 --> 00:23:07.680
Right. And that's a cultural shift.

00:23:07.680 --> 00:23:08.120
You have to

00:23:08.120 --> 00:23:12.520
actually intentionally kind of engineer
and kind of distill down to your team.

00:23:12.680 --> 00:23:14.040
And so that's kind of our default

00:23:14.040 --> 00:23:17.240
now that we're operating at is like, hey,
does anyone have a skill for this?

00:23:17.280 --> 00:23:20.160
If not, we create one together
and we maintain it together.

00:23:20.160 --> 00:23:24.520
Again, I don't want 15 engineers
creating 15 skills for the same thing,

00:23:24.560 --> 00:23:27.840
especially with this world of the token
budget becoming a problem.

00:23:27.920 --> 00:23:30.200
That's just extremely wasteful
engineering, right?

00:23:30.200 --> 00:23:32.360
We should all be trying to share
when we can.

00:23:32.360 --> 00:23:33.360
We have coding agents.

00:23:33.360 --> 00:23:33.960
There's no reason

00:23:33.960 --> 00:23:37.240
that we can't really all work together
and have our agents maintain it.

00:23:37.280 --> 00:23:39.960
As long as we have evals that define
what the behavior should be.

00:23:39.960 --> 00:23:42.040
This is actually
much easier than it sounds.

00:23:43.320 --> 00:23:43.600
Okay,

00:23:43.600 --> 00:23:46.560
so what do you do when you go back to work
Monday or maybe tomorrow?

00:23:46.560 --> 00:23:47.040
Whatever.

00:23:47.040 --> 00:23:48.480
Least me, I'm going back Monday.

00:23:48.480 --> 00:23:50.880
But you want to just start small.

00:23:50.880 --> 00:23:53.880
You don't need to go build
some crazy robust platform like we did

00:23:53.880 --> 00:23:56.520
that that came from like two years of us
beating our head off a wall, right.

00:23:56.520 --> 00:23:58.680
So really wanted something more robust.

00:23:58.680 --> 00:24:00.480
But you don't have to start there.

00:24:00.480 --> 00:24:01.600
Start with one workflow,

00:24:01.600 --> 00:24:03.800
one concept,
one thing that you find yourself

00:24:03.800 --> 00:24:04.480
explaining over

00:24:04.480 --> 00:24:07.680
and over to different audiences
and just turn that into a skill

00:24:07.720 --> 00:24:09.280
or a package,
whatever you want to call it.

00:24:09.280 --> 00:24:11.320
But really, at the end of the day,
an agent skill

00:24:11.320 --> 00:24:14.400
and really just give thought to, okay,
who are the audiences?

00:24:14.400 --> 00:24:16.240
What platforms are they operate on?

00:24:16.240 --> 00:24:20.600
Can I maintain the Readme here and sync
to those registries or whatever it is?

00:24:20.640 --> 00:24:22.560
That should be kind of your objective.

00:24:22.560 --> 00:24:24.320
Add evaluations.

00:24:24.320 --> 00:24:24.880
That is key.

00:24:24.880 --> 00:24:26.960
If you don't do
that, it's just going to fall apart.

00:24:26.960 --> 00:24:29.080
So make sure you define the bare minimum.

00:24:29.080 --> 00:24:31.760
What do I expect this skill
to actually help people with?

00:24:31.760 --> 00:24:34.280
And how do I actually validate
that? That's it.

00:24:34.280 --> 00:24:35.840
Just give a little thought to that up
front.

00:24:35.840 --> 00:24:38.920
It'll go a long way and you can kind of
compound on that over time.

00:24:39.200 --> 00:24:41.040
And then of course start sharing it.

00:24:41.040 --> 00:24:44.640
But you don't want to just share,
you know, with everyone right away.

00:24:44.680 --> 00:24:46.800
You kind of use it yourself first.

00:24:46.800 --> 00:24:48.680
This is like the 0.0.

00:24:48.680 --> 00:24:50.040
x version of the context, right?

00:24:50.040 --> 00:24:53.800
You really do want to kind of semantically
version your skills and your context

00:24:53.840 --> 00:24:56.200
to give signal to your engineers
consuming it. Right?

00:24:56.200 --> 00:24:59.680
Because if I go look at a skill
and it's version 1.0, I expect it to do

00:24:59.720 --> 00:25:02.760
exactly what it's supposed to do
on the first try with almost no friction.

00:25:02.800 --> 00:25:03.120
Right?

00:25:03.120 --> 00:25:05.840
And if it doesn't do that,
you've lost my trust, right?

00:25:05.840 --> 00:25:08.080
So signify that with your versioning.

00:25:08.080 --> 00:25:10.200
How mature is that actually right.

00:25:10.200 --> 00:25:13.600
So start with version zero zero,
share it with a few, get some feedback.

00:25:13.640 --> 00:25:14.240
Iterate.

00:25:14.240 --> 00:25:17.560
Once it's becoming a little more stable,
maybe zero dot x, right?

00:25:17.600 --> 00:25:21.240
And then finally, once you've shared it
with a lot more people and it's regularly

00:25:21.280 --> 00:25:23.320
like people are like, man,
this is working great for me.

00:25:23.320 --> 00:25:25.520
That's a good signal
to move it to version one, right?

00:25:25.520 --> 00:25:28.120
And then that gives everyone else
a signal, hey, you can consume this.

00:25:28.120 --> 00:25:29.080
Here's what the skill does.

00:25:29.080 --> 00:25:32.240
I promise it'll work
because it's battle tested and evaluated.

00:25:32.720 --> 00:25:37.000
So takeaways here again our agent
architectures they change every day.

00:25:37.000 --> 00:25:39.040
You'll have a new one next week.
Next month.

00:25:39.040 --> 00:25:40.200
You really can't predict that.

00:25:40.200 --> 00:25:42.720
But your context is
your durable investment right?

00:25:42.720 --> 00:25:47.520
Really spend time on how you contacts
engineer and maintain things in one place,

00:25:47.520 --> 00:25:50.520
but distribute that to multiple systems
to keep them in sync.

00:25:50.560 --> 00:25:52.200
That's really
where you want to spend your time

00:25:52.200 --> 00:25:55.200
and just build your pipelines today,
because everything else is definitely

00:25:55.200 --> 00:25:56.600
going to change on you anyway.

00:25:56.600 --> 00:25:59.600
So hopefully this was useful information
for everyone.

00:25:59.920 --> 00:26:02.480
And thank you for listening
to me yet for a while.

